import { CorrectionCopy } from "../../correction-model"
pub fn copy -> CorrectionCopy = CorrectionCopy {
  summary: "Return an updated accumulator and ask reduceUntil to continue.",
  taskTitle: "Pass the updated total and continue",
  purpose: "Next value constructs the decision to continue with that value as the new accumulator, or running total. Here step adds a score and returns the updated total with Next. reduceUntil reads that decision and moves to the next element with that total.",
  setup: "import * as collections makes std/collection available as collections. fn defines a function; calls separate arguments with spaces. Int means an integer, String means text, and Array<Int> means an array of integers; [] is empty.",
  codeTitle: "Add scores until a negative marker",
  outputTitle: "Seseragi output",
  after: [
    "In step, total: Int is the current total and score: Int is the score just read. -> collections.ReduceStep<Int> means it returns a continue-or-stop decision carrying an integer total. The arrows separate the two parameters, supplied as total and then score, from that returned type.",
    "If score < 0 is true, the function returns collections.Done total. Otherwise collections.Next (total + score) carries the updated total. Next and Done are the two named alternatives of ReduceStep. Both carry one integer here; neither constructor name is itself the result type to write in the annotation.",
    "collections.reduceUntil 0 step [10, 20, -1, 100] receives the initial zero, the step function, and the array in that order. Ten and twenty make 30; -1 returns Done 30, so 100 is not processed. Without a negative marker, [10, 20] also finishes at 30. Empty input returns the initial zero without calling step.",
    "carried reads a decision with match. Its collections.Next value and collections.Done value branches each name and return the integer inside. After extracting 99 from Done 99, the program still prints still running. Constructing Done does not end the program or main.",
  ],
  comparisonTitle: "Compare the same task in TypeScript",
  comparisonBefore: "For this task alone, an ordinary TypeScript for...of loop with break is concise; the program prints that total first. It then compares returning a decision from a separate function. The | in the type permits two object shapes with kind equal to next or done; both carry the total in value. reduceUntil checks kind to decide whether to continue or return value.",
  comparisonAfter: "The first TypeScript 30 comes from the ordinary loop; the next comes from processing the same input with the decision callback. The remaining 30, 0, 99 and still running cover the same cases as Seseragi. In either language, constructing a decision does not stop the surrounding program. Seseragi supplies the callback result type and consumer in the standard library; this does not mean a simple loop always becomes shorter.",
  detailTitle: "Type guarantees and common mistakes",
  details: [
    "ReduceStep<Int> is different from an ordinary integer. Returning only total + score from step produces SES-T0101. Return an explicit decision such as Next (total + score) to continue or Done total to stop. Annotating an integer does not convert it into a decision.",
    "In ReduceStep<A>, A names the carried result type. It is Int here, but the input element and result types need not always match. Next and Done carry the same result type in one calculation. The declaration name arg1 denotes the single value argument supplied to a constructor.",
    "reduceUntil is the consumer that interprets Done and stops the reduction. After Done, it neither pulls another element nor calls step again. It cannot undo expressions already evaluated to build the input array. If every decision is Next it returns the final total; on empty input it returns the initial value.",
    "The work is proportional to the number of elements visited before stopping. reduceUntil also accepts an Iterator that may be endless; if neither Done nor the end is reached, it will not finish. This example uses a finite array. Its small-integer comparison does not equate all TypeScript numbers with Int.",
  ],
}
